Here is what a complete multi-domain, path-routed Ingress manifest looks like in production:

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: shopzone-ingress
  annotations:
    # URL rewriting: strip prefix before forwarding to backend container
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /$2
    # Enforce SSL redirection
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true"
spec:
  # Links this rule to the cluster's Nginx Ingress Controller
  ingressClassName: nginx

  # TLS Termination (HTTPS on port 443)
  tls:
  - hosts:
      - shopzone.com
      - api.shopzone.com
    secretName: shopzone-tls-cert  # Secret containing tls.crt and tls.key

  rules:
  # Route 1: Path-based routing under the main website domain
  - host: shopzone.com
    http:
      paths:
      # Traffic to shopzone.com/products goes to product microservice
      - path: /products(/|$)(.*)
        pathType: ImplementationSpecific
        backend:
          service:
            name: product-service
            port:
              number: 8080

      # Traffic to shopzone.com/cart goes to checkout microservice
      - path: /cart(/|$)(.*)
        pathType: ImplementationSpecific
        backend:
          service:
            name: cart-service
            port:
              number: 3000

  # Route 2: Host-based (subdomain) routing
  - host: api.shopzone.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: backend-api-service
            port:
              number: 5000


Step-by-Step Request Lifecycle

When a user visits [https://shopzone.com/cart](https://shopzone.com/cart):

1.DNS Resolution: The client browser queries DNS for shopzone.com and receives the external IP of the Cloud Load Balancer.

2.Edge Ingestion: The Cloud Load Balancer forwards the raw TCP packet on port 443 to the ingress-nginx-controller Pod running on a worker node.

3.TLS Termination: The Ingress Controller decrypts the HTTPS traffic using the certificate found in shopzone-tls-cert.

4.Header Evaluation: The controller inspects the HTTP request:

  1.Host header matches shopzone.com.

  2.URI path matches /cart.

5.Upstream Resolution: The controller checks the internal EndpointSlice for cart-service to retrieve available pod IPs (e.g., 10.244.1.18:3000).

6.Reverse Proxying: The controller forwards the HTTP request to the selected container, receives the response, and streams it back to the user’s browser.